<span>test_chroma/</span>
<span>*.sqlite3</span></span></code></div></div></div><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>Your </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">claims.json</span><span>, </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">reality.json</span><span>, and </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">findings.json</span><span> are different. If they're useful as </span><strong class="font-semibold"><span>example demo outputs</span></strong><span>, you can keep them, although eventually I'd organize them under something like </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">demo_outputs/</span><span>.</span></p><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>Also keep </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">seed_demo_data.py</span><span>. That's what makes your intentional demo scenario reproducible without uploading the Chroma database.</span></p><h2 class="Heading-Ro3lZr" dir="auto"><span>Now, how to merge your current branch into </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">main</span></h2><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>First </span><strong class="font-semibold"><span>don't merge yet</span></strong><span> until your current work is committed.</span></p><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>In your VS Code terminal, run:</span></p><div class="CodeBlock-zu1QM3"><div data-markdown-copy="code-block" class="relative w-full min-w-0 overflow-clip contain-inline-size rounded-(--radius-3xl-base) border border-subtle bg-secondary-soft-alpha Surface-dfq13n dark @container" data-theme="dark" data-search-result-target=""><div data-markdown-copy="exclude" class="flex items-center font-sans