3203 3.89237 7.33203 4.85059V5.95215H10.9336C12.6262 5.95229 13.998 7.32491 13.998 9.01758V12.6182H15.1006C16.0584 12.6178 16.8348 11.8416 16.835 10.8838V4.85059C16.835 3.89262 16.0585 3.11564 15.1006 3.11523H9.06738Z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></button></span></div></div></div><div class="text-size-chat overflow-auto p-2 outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-inset px-4 pt-0 pb-3 md:px-5 chatgpt-code-scrollport InlineCodePane-EYx4bd" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! block text-size-code CodeContent-ybJ8Tl min-w-max"><span><span>documentation_consistency.json</span></span></code></div></div></div><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>If you merge/commit before it finishes, you could capture an incomplete or missing output.</span></p><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>So safest order:</span></p><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><strong class="font-semibold"><span>Let agent finish → check output → commit your branch → merge into </span><span data-markdown-copy="inline-code" class="inline-markdown InlineMarkdownIsolate-QWDV4W" dir="ltr">main</span><span>.</span></strong><span> ✅</span></p><p class="Paragraph-kKnbIo" dir="auto"><span>You can open a </span><strong class="font-semibold"><span>second terminal</span></strong><span> in VS Code if you want to work on Git commands meanwhile, but don't commit the generated result until the agent finishes.</span></p></div></div></div><span hidden=""></span></div></div><div class="mt-1.5 flex turn-action-controls min-h-5 min-w-0 max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-wrap items-center justify-start gap-0.5 browser:-ms-0.5 browser:mt-3 electron:-translate-x-1 extension:-translate-x-1.5 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-2 [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-ring [&amp;_button]:focus-visible:ring-offset-0 [--side-tooltip:bottom]"><div class="flex min-h-5 min-w-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-0.5 browser:gap-0"><span data-state="closed" class="contents"><button type